1 OCCU Ridder af Elefantordenen 1563, Riksråd, Ridder til Elefantordenen og Lensmand, Lensmand
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Jørgen Ottesen Rosenkrantz=
2 CONT
2 CONT * In April 1559, Jørgen Rosenkrantz acquired the farm Holm outside Hornslet from the King. In the following years, he built a castle on that spot and named it Rosenholm after his family.
2 CONT * Jørgen Rosenkrantz, also known as Jørgen Ottesen Rosenkrantz , 14.5.1523-9.4.1596, Danish councilor; brother of Erik Ottesen Rosenkrantz and Holger Ottesen Rosenkrantz . Rosenkrantz became closely associated with the Reformation
2 CONC  in his youth, first as a student of the reformer Frands Vormordsen in Malmö, then as a student at the universities of Copenhagen and Wittenberg. From 1548 he was in the personal service of Elector Moritz of Saxony , until he was 
2 CONC attached to the Danish court in 1552. Rosenkrantz held several sheriff's posts and in 1563 became a member of the council of the kingdom. From 1588 until his death he was a member of Christian IV's guardian government, and upon th
2 CONC e death of his friend Chancellor Niels Kaas in 1594 he became a leading member of the council of the kingdom. Rosenkrantz was very concerned with church affairs and warmly supported young students and scientists. As the youngest s
2 CONC on, he only inherited scattered property, but built the main estates Rosenholm and Skaføgård himself .
2 CONT * The family Rosenkrantz has owned the castle ever since, and today Rosenholm is the Danish house which has been in the possession of the same family for the longest time. During the period 1740-45, the castle was renovated and th
2 CONC e large park covering approx. 5 hectares were laid out complete with lime tree avenues and bowers and beech hedges.
2 CONT * In 1574 the Danish nobleman and humanist Jørgen Rosenkrantz (d. 1596) commissioned an altarpiece for his parish church in Hornslet, located in eastern Jylland (or Jutland). The altarpiece was assembled by an itinerant Netherland
2 CONC ish sculptor, Albret Gluxmester, from two separate pre-Reformation altarpieces – one German and wooden, the other English and alabaster – with a number of new alabaster additions and repairs made by Gluxmester himself. Around the 
2 CONC same time, Rosenkrantz arranged for the exhumation and transfer of the long-buried bodies of fourteen generations of his family from multiple locations across Denmark to the church, where he had them reburied. He also commissioned
2 CONC  new monuments to mark their presence within the church. These linked instances of recycling and reburial prompt us to think about the temporalities of reuse in Denmark in the wake of the Protestant Reformation. Rosenkrantz witnes
2 CONC sed the establishment of Lutheranism as the dominant religion of Denmark in his own lifetime. As one of the most powerful men in the country, serving on the council of Frederik II, of Denmark, and taking a leading role in the Rege
2 CONC ncy period after the King's death, he would have his own ideological part to play in this.1 The Hornslet altarpiece both enriches our understanding of Rosenkrantz's relationship to the past and by extension a particular attitude t
2 CONC owards temporality embedded within Danish Lutheranism, and invites us to consider medieval English alabasters in their wider European context.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.visitaarhus.com/aarhus-region/plan-your-trip/rosenholm-slot-gdk1078933 Rosenholm Slot]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Castle'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The rose garden surrounding the fountain is an enchanting place. The castle is fully furnished with hundreds of items of furniture, paintings, and tapestries, which provide the visitor with a fascinating view of a noble family’s l
2 CONC ife through centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today Rosenholm Castle unites past and present culture as the organiser of exciting tours, conferences, company parties, weddings, etc. The castle is one of the biggest and most beautiful Danish manors from the Renaissance.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ridder af Elefantordenen 1563.
2 CONT
2 CONT =ROSENHOLM SLOTS's HISTORIE:=  
2 CONT
2 CONT The east wing was built first. It is the tallest of the four wings, and is flanked by two domed towers. The remarkable thing about that wing is that it was originally built with an open loggia (colonnade) facing the courtyard. How
2 CONC ever, it was quickly bricked up - probably after the first Danish winter. One must still admire that the architect was able to sell the idea of ​​an open loggia to the Danish knight Jørgen. If you stand in the courtyard, you can s
2 CONC till clearly see where the openings were.
2 CONT
2 CONT The west wing was built around 1570, meaning that the two wings were not connected at the beginning. A couple of times across the courtyard on a cold winter morning was probably one of the reasons why this was changed relatively q
2 CONC uickly, and within the next ten years the two side wings were added. In 1580 Rosenholm thus had the shape that we still see today.
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate today: In the 1930s, the estate, which until the death of Hans Lensbaron Rosenkrantz, the director of the National Bank, had been upheld as a gathering place for the family's great and precious traditions, completely fe
2 CONC ll into disrepair and the forests and the breeding farm were sold off and Rosenholm gained its current size of 33 hectares of park, meadow and forest.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Lensbaroness Carin Rosenkrantz in 1996, Rosenholm passed from the family to the Rosenholm Foundation, which today has Christian Rosenkrantz as chairman of the board and Baron Erik Christian Rosenkrantz who today
2 CONC  acts as general manager - so the family continues to influence the development of the place to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Restorations: Contrary to the exterior, Rosenholm has undergone major changes over time. The interior decoration is mainly due to Iver Rosenkrantz (1674 - 1745) who had the main building renovated from basement to ceiling, had the
2 CONC  park redeveloped and had the magnificent linen floors planted.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 19th century, Rosenholm underwent a rather heavy-handed restoration, where, among other things, the limestone used in the castle was replaced with cement. Cement was a new and exciting product at that time. At the small pav
2 CONC ilion in the park where the limestone has been preserved, you can get an idea of ​​what it looked like in its time. Furthermore, all the farms that bordered the castle were demolished and gathered south of the courtyard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Holger and Carin Rosenkrantz, major maintenance work has been carried out. The roof was replaced in the 1960s and the bridge over the moat was also completely renovated. In the late 1990s, Rosenholm underwent a number of imp
2 CONC rovements under the current management and today stands as a fine example of how, despite difficult times for castles and manors, it is possible to preserve part of Danish cultural heritage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rosenkrantz: The Rosenkrantz family is both one of the oldest and wealthiest Danish noble families. As is the case with several medieval families, the earliest history is surrounded by legends. The Rosenkrantz family has thus ofte
2 CONC n been seen in connection with the Stygge family. These two families are said to have the same common ancestor, - Prince Stygge in 650. However, the family can be traced back to 1308 to Knight Iver.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family name Rosenkrantz: Knight Erik of Hveringeholm is said to have gone to Rome around 1350 and received the rosary from the Pope, which later gave the family its name. (The rosary is not depicted in the coat of arms itself,
2 CONC  but is located as a wreath in the horns, on the helmet that is often shown in connection with the coat of arms.) However, the family name did not become common until 1524, after Frederick I's decree to adopt fixed family names.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rosenkrantzs and their significance: From the end of the 15th century, the family was a permanent part of the political elite in Denmark and is one of the families that has provided the most members to the Council of State and
2 CONC  other leading positions in political life. From the first years of Christian IV's life until he came of age, it was Jørgen Rosenkrantz (1523 - 1596) who effectively ruled Denmark in collaboration with the two other noble families
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Rosenkrantzs on Rosenholm Jørgen Rosenkrantz was also the person who started Rosenholm in 1559. His son Holger (1572 - 1642) also called "learned Holger" is probably one of the most famous members of the family. In addit
2 CONC ion to being prime minister for a period, a leading cultural personality, he educated the sons of other noble families at his small university out in the park called "Pirkentavl". This small pavilion is considered the first univer
2 CONC sity in Jutland. It is difficult to describe the development of the family through history in a few words, but it is characteristic that it continued to hold important and political offices right up until the last century. The las
2 CONC t important post was held by the lord baron, chamberlain, court hunter Hans Carl Oluf Rosenkrantz (1870 - 1936), who served as director of the National Bank.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family until today: Lensbaron Hans, Carl Oluf Rosenkrantz, entered into two marriages: from the first marriage - he had a son Lensbaron Holger Julius Frederik Knud Lavard Josef til Rosenholm (1902 - 1975) who was the last Lens
2 CONC baron
2 CONT
