2 CONT Definition of (Sysselmann.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sysselmann/Sýslumaður is a Norwegian and Icelandic title of local government. It was used during the Middle Ages as a noble title. It has been revived twice in modern times as a special form of local government, the Governor of Sv
2 CONC albard now holds the title and the Governor of Erik the Red's Land held the title from 1932 to 1933. The English version is sheriff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Definition of Riksråd
2 CONT
2 CONT (Riksråd) is in Scandinavia and a title on a royal adviser (COUNCILOR), and the collection of these advisers. Ursprunget till riksrådet var att kungen, omgiven av stormän i sin närhet, naturligt inhämtade dessas råd och stöd vid b
2 CONC eslut. The origin of the Swedish Council was that the king, surrounded by great men in their vicinity, naturally sought their advice and support in the decision.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rådet utvecklades under 1200-talet. It was developed in the 1200s. Där ingick biskoparna (ärkebiskopen var lagfäst som ledamot), stormän och lagmän (ofta samma personer). Where were the bishops (Archbishop was legal as a member), 
2 CONC great and lagmän (often the same people). Rådet ansåg sig som medlare mellan kung och folk. The Council considered as mediators between the king and people. Dessa kom med tiden att betraktas som en särskild korporation i riket. Th
2 CONC ose came with time to be considered as a separate corporation in the kingdom. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Norwegian Riksråd gradually emerged around 1300, evolving from the King's council. It emerged clearly as a power factor after 1319, during the minority of king Magnus VII Eriksson. There doesn't seem to have been any clear rul
2 CONC es for how many members the council should have, or who should be councillors. During the 1400s, the number could be from 30 to 40, whereas after 1500 it was barely above 10. The Norwegian bishops were automatically members. There
2 CONC  was at the time five bishops in mainland Norway (the archbishop in Trondheim, the bishops in Oslo, Hamar, Stavanger, Bergen), plus one in the Faroe Islands, two on Iceland, one in Greenland, and one in Orkney (lost to Scotland in
2 CONC  1468). The bishops from the islands rarely participated in the Norwegian riksråd. In addition, the chaplains of the royal chapels in Oslo and Bergen were permanent members. So were the commanders of the five strongest castles of 
2 CONC Norway: Bohus, Akershus in Oslo, the castles in Bergen, Tønsberg and Trondheim. The rest of the council was recruited from the Norwegian nobility. Only Norwegians were eligible for these places in the Norwegian Riksråd - however, 
2 CONC this was also taken to include foreigners who had married a Norwegian. As several of the bishops and commanders of the castles were foreign - mostly Danish, but also some Swedish and German - the number of Norwegians in the Norweg
2 CONC ian Riksråd gradually diminished. The archbishop of Trondheim mostly acted as the head of the council.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Norway had of old been a hereditary kingdom, the Norwegian Riksråd's task did not, originally include the election of the king, as in Sweden and Denmark. However, as successive kings died without leaving any issue, starting wit
2 CONC h Olav IV in 1387, it fell to the Riksråd to interpret the succession laws. This it did so freely that Norway more and more became, in reality, an elected monarchy, like its Nordic neighbours. This was formally affirmed in 1450, w
2 CONC hen Christian I of Denmark took the Norwegian throne as an elected monarch. On his death, in 1481, the Riksråd ruled the country for two years, in an interregnum, before electing Christian's son as the new king - a period which co
2 CONC uld be seen as the height of the council's power.
2 CONT
