1 NOTE {geni:about_me} NOT TO BE MERGED WITH GYLDENHORN!
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding Mrs. Gudrun's son Olav Eriksson to Tronstad, Per Reidar Christiansen writes in Genealogen 1/2005 pages 37 - 55 (Norwegian Genealogical Society):
2 CONT
2 CONT "The assumption that he (Olav Eriksson of Tronstad) belonged to the Gyldenhorn family was for a long time generally accepted as a fact until it was emphatically dethroned in 1936 in volume X of the Norwegian Genealogical Journal. 
2 CONC Nicolay Stene then proved that Olav's mother was married to the armorer Erik Stensson of the Swedish salvation family Natt och Dag. Consequently, he assumed that this was the father (of Olav Eriksson)"
2 CONT
2 CONT "Henning Sollied (Sollied 1940, pp. 271-276) correctly pointed out that the inheritance of Erik Stensson must have meant that he left no surviving heirs. Consequently, Olav Eriksson in Tronstad could not have been his son, but rat
2 CONC her an unknown Erik. Since Olav's mother, Gudrun Håkansdotter, also held the title of wife, despite Erik Stensson never reaching the rank of knight, Sollied concluded that she had also been married to a knight. Sollied suggested t
2 CONC hat the unknown knight may have been Olav Eriksson's father, but did not rule out that Olav was born in a third marriage, possibly out of wedlock."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Regardless of whether he (Olav Eriksson of Tronstad) was a legitimate son or not, his mother and her family are known, his father is unknown. The only conclusion we can draw with certainty from the flimsy and ambiguous source mat
2 CONC erial is that it does not allow us to draw solid conclusions."
