2 CONC gewalde 1 norrena and silde to Dalafogden Jösse Finsson 1400%): eat a tikt Dafinsarwit üstan Erik Skinnare sold to Stora Nissa 14491) ; another quarry Baambara arfwit wihr Borem (oden part of the property Norslund, which is locate
2 CONC d at the North, where Tisken flows into Runn>). as underlagman Korske exchanged for him by Ingewalde 1 norrena and silde to Dalafogden Jösse Finsson 1400%): eat a tikt Dafinsarwit üstan tiskisse oden part of Kungsgärden Norets ego
2 CONC r invid Falu stad, sik-sar om Tisken>), which Dagfind Dagfindsson, bergsman 1351, ürft after his father, who cultivated it E. F. 1925 - he was one of the Germans who during the 12th and 14th centuries practiced IMAGE 181. HARALDSB
2 CONC O, MANBYGGNADEN'S EDGE. mining at Kopparberget.
2 CONT Dagfind d. y's daughter Walburgh and her husband Jowan Dafinzmager (måg) or Jon Daghfintz i daghfinzarweno i nore mortgaged the property in 1400 to Jüsse Finsson, who received a fastebrer dirpa in 1406*): they also owed him copper
2 CONC s and money, which he paid from their vineyards to Lafrinze ilyttokiempason and Hindrik Symbla *). '''The name occurs later than a gang, in a salubrious book in 1528 by Anders Olsson l Noret, which lists, among other things, the D
2 CONC affijnsserwet to the judge Per Sijwardsson at Frümby and his wife Barbara (Hjort af Ornäs), whom Anders calls mijn nesthe frenka*). According to a purchase and two fastebrer from 1400, all in Grycksbo gärdsarkix. Jöns Wibförnsson 
2 CONC and his children Ingebjörn and Gudlög then sold a farm at Noret on Kopparberget to Margwart Frowloff, whom we met before at Korsnüs: the deed of sale is dated elocka odhensdagh, i.e. Dymmelonsdagen in the Easter recess, when the c
2 CONC hurch bells' clappers are tied so as not to make a sound*).
2 CONT And Kzetilbiörn in Noor is mentioned as a twelve-year-old good home in a parchment document of 1414, also in the Grycksbo archive.''' In 1539, Erik Alffsson and Nils in Noredt were fourth-party owners, the former in the 1510s
2 CONT also a judge and a seer (his grandson with the same name was ennobled Ikorn). In 1549, Hustru Karljn / Noredt is mentioned, in 1569 a farm Lunden nira täktekarls. and the mining estate of No-ret. In 1571 Päuel Mänsson, Anders Pers
2 CONC son, Lasse Oloffsson, Hinrlick Finne taxed. Wife Elin and Wife Anna in Norett - the latter had gold, silver, copper, müissing, tin. + cows and 13 goats, all valued at 732 marks 3 üre. 1612 taxpayers were Hans and
2 CONT east 1 Nor: Jöns 1 Nore (Jöns Persson, syneman 1614) and Ingel & Nohret each left his sölfskied, Clas Döpken a sölfkosa and Christopher Olsson in Nohret S R:dal:r. The "noble mining estate" Christopher Olofsson in Nohr is mentione
2 CONC d above (son of bailiff Olof Jonsson at Borns Hyttegärd and brother of Nils Olofsson at Främsbacka), b. 1510, churchwarden, great mining entrepreneur, had privileges from the King to mint his own copper, attended the lord's day in
2 CONC  Stockholm in 1604, bought the property Nohr from the miner Per Larsson in 1608, made donations to Kopparberg's church as well as to Sundborn's*) and was ennobled in 16219) D. D. 105. The Hena or Heynesarvet estate lies a good dis
2 CONC tance into the present-day town of Falu, see the first book.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the nickname Gyllen-knosters because he gave advances to the crown and admonished and commanded others to do the same, as it is stated in his long royal charter'), which according to Hülpher's notes was the only evidence avai
2 CONC lable about the Bergs Adelen in Sweden": there he speaks of Bergsadell in the past and his ancestors and his shield is established: a blue mountaineer in a yellow field with his burning lamp on his head, in his left hand a hook K.
2 CONC  B. F. 1925 PICTURE 182. KALKBOD WITH STRAW ROOF, HARALDSBO. wa on the hook and break the mountain, and on the helmet three knösters with a gold handle in a cross". However, he never took an introduction - if a savior died without
2 CONC  male heirs, his estate would go to the crown, so he renounced the nobility, but had the coat of arms attached with a gold chain to the crossbeam in Kopparberg church, from which it was, however, taken down when the choir was expa
2 CONC nded in 1685. On a pillar in the church he also erected his epitaph in 1618 - he himself died in 1628 -, where Wälachtad Christopher Olofsson in Noohr is depicted with his two first wives, 5 sons and 3 daughters, all deceased*). T
2 CONC he first wife Malin, born in 1595, was the daughter of the commander of Born's Hyttegärd Persson in Varggärden, the second was Daniel Thorkilsson Swinhufwud's widow in Frim-by Brita Mänsdotter (Roos af Hjelmsäter), 1 1614 - the th
2 CONC ird's name letters are found on the epitaph »foot zirat«: there it says ›Christopher O. S. and C. A. D. 1618», which is consistent with both Jacob Ingelsson's and Hammarström's as well as Schlegel-Klingspor's information, that Chr
2 CONC istopher Olsson's third and last wife was Carin Andersdotter Räf from Hyttnäs in Sundborn, then remarried to the mountaineer Johan Eriksson there and with him mother of assessor Johan Silfverstöm's wife - on Johan Briksson's grave
2 CONC stone in Sundborn's church his wife is also named Carin Andersdotter, † 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate register after Christopher Olsson includes, among other things: a. then planting hill on eight posts. So a small plant nursery for raising seedlings for
2 CONT 1) Printed by Schlegel cch Klingspor p. 158, where he is incorrectly stated as the son of Olof Christophersson at Rottneby as well as in D. D., Geneal. Tab, and eni. them in my Flodabok.
2 CONT 3) Schlegel-Klingspor's list of children, according to which Nigra should have lived after
2 CONT 1618, must therefore be partly incorrect. Hans Christophersson was thus not the son of Christopher Olsson, but of Bergson Christopher Hansson 1 Nygärden 1 Wika (according to Helmer Lagergren).
2 CONT
