1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =='''Siffri (Sippora) Pedersdatter (c. 1569 - c. 1642)'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hypothesis of Noble Descent:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Her '''probable''' descent from '''Peder Eilersen Brockenhuus''' (lord of [https://lokalhistoriewiki.no/wiki/Hovinsholm_%28Ringsaker_gnr._535/1%29 Hovinsholm]) is based on several compelling, albeit indirect, legal and social fact
2 CONC ors:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. Social Context (1589 Wedding):'''
2 CONT Sippora's marriage to '''Provost Jens Bugge''' on July 13, 1589, was hosted at [https://snl.no/Tjerne Tjerne] by the nobleman '''[https://www.geni.com/people/J%C3%B8rgen-Kruckow/6000000004648182363 Jørgen Kruckow] (brother-in-law 
2 CONC of Peder Brockenhuus)'''. Kruckow was married to '''Margrete Eilersdatter Brockenhuus''', the sister of Peder Brockenhuus. Thus, Kruckow was presumably Sippora's uncle by marriage (inngiftet onkel). According to the social norms o
2 CONC f the 16th century, this probable family connection explains why Kruckow '"hosted the wedding" (holdt bryllup), acting as the primary patron for his niece. The fact of the wedding between Jens Bugge and Zephretz (Sippora) is confi
2 CONC rmed by record No. 788 in the Hedemarksregisteret (1589). This fact that the wedding was hosted by an influential relative from the Brockenhuus line indicates Sippora's recognition as a member of the high nobility family. At the t
2 CONC ime of the wedding, Jens Bugge (born c. 1542) was approximately 47 years old and already a powerful Provost, reinforcing the view that this was a significant alliance between the high clergy and the Brockenhuus nobility. Furthermo
2 CONC re, the social interaction continued years later. On September 14, 1594, Bishop Jens Nilssøn's visitation records note a dinner hosted at the home of '''Jens Bugge''' and '''Sippora in Ringsaker'''. Among the distinguished guests 
2 CONC present were the same '''Jørgen Kruckow''' and his wife, '''Margrete Brockenhuus''', reinforcing their continued close ties and Sippora's established place within this noble circle. The presence of the High Nobility at a private d
2 CONC inner in a parsonage (prestegård) was a breach of strict social hierarchy unless justified by kinship.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. Property Links (Opsal Estate and Brockenhuus):''' 
2 CONT Following her husband's death in 1612, Sippora managed the significant '''Opsal''' estate in the parish of Veldre, [https://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ringsaker Ringsaker] (Hedmark) for 30 years. According to record No. 1069 in Hedemar
2 CONC ksregisteret (1615), Sippora (Zephora Pedersdatter på Opsall) personally participated in a court hearing (ting) regarding the borders of the Opsal estate. According to record no. 1111 in Hedemarksregisteret (1622), her status as t
2 CONC he widow of '''Jens Bugge''' was officially registered for parts of the Opsal lands. Sippora's possession of these lands as the actual owner further strengthens her integration into the Brockenhuus family. The connection between O
2 CONC psal and the social circle of the Brockenhuus family is further reflected in the anthroponomics of the estate's residents. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the '''1664''' census, the farm was managed by '''Eilert på Opsal''', whose age was recorded as '''80 years''' (b. c. 1584/85). The name Eilert (Eiler) was exceedingly rare in Hedmark at that time and served as a dist
2 CONC inctive marker for the Brockenhuus lineage (the name of Peder Brockenhuus’s father). The successive presence of Sippora and Eilert at Opsal may be interpreted as an indicator of potential family continuity, likely within a non-mar
2 CONC ital branch of the Brockenhuus line.
2 CONT
2 CONT By '''1665''', the estate’s ownership was recorded under '''Margrethe Bjelke''' (Welbåren Jomfru), daughter of Chancellor [https://dev.lokalhistoriewiki.no/index.php?title=Jens_Bjelke&mobileaction=toggle_view_desktop Jens Bjelke] 
2 CONC and '''Sophia Brockenhuus''' (niece of Peder, daughter of his brother '''Henrik'''). In Norwegian allodial law (Odelsrett), this return of the property to the main noble line (tilbakeskyld) after the extinction of a secondary bran
2 CONC ch is a "final proof" that Opsal remained part of the Brockenhuus family's ancestral domain (stamtavlegods) throughout Sippora’s life.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Peder Brockenhuus''' (the '''alleged''' father of Sippora) owned Hovin ([https://lokalhistoriewiki.no/wiki/Hovinsholm_(Ringsaker_gnr._535/1) Hovinsholm]) in Hedmark (around 1600), passing later to his sister '''Margrethe Brocke
2 CONC nhuus''' (who hosted Sippora’s wedding) and eventually in 1637 to '''Sophia Brockenhuus''' and Chancellor '''Jens Bjelke'''. Even though Hovinsholm and Opsal were located relatively close to each other, considering Norway's geogra
2 CONC phy and the administrative divisions of the time, Opsal was not part of the Hovinsholm estate grounds (as they are geographically separated), but it was part of the Brockenhuus family's landholdings (gods). Sippora’s residency and
2 CONC  legal defense of rights within this specific family property line and the eventual reversion of the estate directly confirm her blood kinship with the Brockenhuus lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. Odal Rights and Legal Standing:''' 
2 CONT Record No. 1256 in Hedemarksregisteret (1637) confirms that '''Sippora at Opsal (Sephora på Opsahl)''' acted as the sole plaintiff in a lawsuit against '''Ole Grannerud''' regarding hunting rights for birds and elk. In 17th-centur
2 CONC y Norwegian law, the active defense of hunting and land-use rights was directly linked to allodial rights ([https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odelsrett odelsrett]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Specifically, in 1637, the right to hunt elk (big game) was an exclusive prerogative reserved for the King and the nobility. By initiating litigation over elk hunting rights in her own name, Sippora was not merely disputing a boun
2 CONC dary, but asserting an exclusive claim to high-status privileges (regale) inherently tied to the land's ownership. For a commoner widow or a mere tenant (leilending), such a legal position would have been untenable. The court’s ac
2 CONC ceptance of her as a plaintiff in this matter serves as a judicial recognition of her high social standing and her "blood right" to the Brockenhuus family’s ancestral privileges. The fact that Sippora personally defended these rig
2 CONC hts on lands forming part of the Brockenhuus family's ancestral domain (stamtavlegods) provides strong evidence of her blood kinship with this family, positioning her as a legitimate protector of that domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4. Onomastic Evidence:''' 
2 CONT The names of Sippora’s sons - '''Peder''' (after her probable father Peder Brockenhuus), along with '''Erik''' and '''Henrik''' (after her probable paternal uncles, Peder's brothers) - strictly follow the naming traditions of the 
2 CONC Brockenhuus family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, the naming patterns of the next generation at Opsal provide critical evidence of an elite cultural identity. Records from the 1664–1666 census show that Eilert på Opsal (Sippora’s likely brother/relative) named his so
2 CONC ns '''Samuel''' and '''Bertil'''. As noted in scholarly discussions (e.g., Arkivverket Forum), the name '''Samuel''' was exceedingly rare in Hedmark at that time and functioned as a "learned" or "clerical" name (prestnavn), typica
2 CONC l of the high-ranking Bugge-Brockenhuus milieu but entirely alien to the local peasantry (bondeslekt). The use of such distinct, non-traditional names serves as a cultural signature, proving that the residents of Opsal were not me
2 CONC re farmers but belonged to an educated, high-status social stratum connected by blood and tradition to the noble and clerical elite.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, during a scholarly discussion on the Arkivverket Forum, the renowned historian and genealogist Tore H. Vigerust emphasized the extreme rarity of the name '''Zephora/Zephori'''. Vigerust noted that the name is entirely
2 CONC  absent from traditional Norwegian name books of the era, which underscores Sippora’s upbringing within an elite, highly educated social circle, likely linked to her mother’s lineage and the high-ranking clerical-noble milieu.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''5. Conclusion on Lineage and Status:'''
2 CONT While Sippora is not listed among the legitimate heirs of Peder Brockenhuus (who officially died childless with his estates passing to his niece Sophia), the cumulative evidence suggests she was his '''non-marital daughter'''. Her
2 CONC  absence from official noble lineages (Adelsstamtavler) of the period is explained by strict social codes: non-marital children (frillebarn) were barred from using the father’s surname or coat of arms to prevent the fragmentation 
2 CONC of the primary allodial inheritance. Providing Sippora with the Opsal estate and delegating her exclusive hunting rights was a form of "social contract" - a provision that guaranteed her elite status without granting legal claims 
2 CONC to the main Brockenhuus ancestral seats. In the socio-legal context of 16th-century Norway, her lifelong possession of the Opsal estate, her active defense of hunting rights (odelsrett) traditionally held by the Brockenhuus line, 
2 CONC and her elite social standing (manifested at her 1589 wedding at Tjerne) are consistent with a provision made for a recognized, albeit illegitimate, noble child. This status is further cemented by the strict adherence to Brockenhu
2 CONC us naming traditions for her sons, a privilege typically reserved for close kin within the high nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus, Sippora Pedersdatter serves as a pivotal 'missing link' between the fading high nobility of the late Middle Ages (the Brockenhuus line) and the emerging elite of officials and clergy (the Bugge dynasty) that shaped Norwegian
2 CONC  society in the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT As for the descendants of the residents of Opsal in the parish of Veldre, they maintained the social environment established by Sippora. The continuity of the "Opsal branch" status is traceable in the late 17th century. According 
2 CONC to historical data (1697), '''Eilert’s grandsons''' - '''Brynild''' and '''Arne Samuelsen''' (sons of '''Samuel Eilertsen, born at Opsal''') - are listed as professional military men (soldiers). Military service was traditional fo
2 CONC r the line of Eilert's descendants, which, combined with the rare family onomastics, confirms the family's preservation of a special social position, characteristic of the descendants of the serving elite.
2 CONT
2 CONT
