1 OCCU Borgmästare i Trondheim, Prest i Herjedalen og i From i Trondheim 1611-1619
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jørgen Gram''' (born in the 1500s – died before July 10, 1657) was a well-educated Danish-born merchant, landowner, and burgomaster (mayor) of Trondheim during the early 17th century. He later served as an admin
2 CONC istrator of the castle law (forvalter av slottsloven) in the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Personal Life and Origins ===
2 CONT Jørgen Gram was born in Gram, Denmark, during the 16th century. He belonged to a prominent clerical family, being the son of the parish priest Reverend Niels in Gram. Since his father, grandfather, and brother (Reverend Hans in Ra
2 CONC vede, Sweden) were all priests, Jørgen was highly literate and well-schooled. The information regarding his lineage is preserved in Gerhard Schønning's genealogical register of the Trygge family. An estate settlement (skifte) foll
2 CONC owing his death was held on July 10, 1657.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Career ===
2 CONT * '''Burgher and Burgomaster:''' He first appears in historical records in 1609 as a burgher in Trondheim. Unlike many contemporary officials, there is no record of him serving as an alderman (rådmann) prior to his mayoral appoint
2 CONC ment. He is first titled as burgomaster on August 21, 1611, and his tenure is heavily documented throughout the 1610s (including in 1612, 1616, and 1619).
2 CONT * '''Retirement:''' His final appearance as active burgomaster in the fief accounts (lensregnskapet) covers the years 1620/21. By 1621, he is referred to as the "former burgomaster," indicating he stepped down during the course of
2 CONC  1620.
2 CONT * '''Other Offices:''' Gram served as the churchwarden (kirkeverge) for Our Lady's Church (Vår Frue kirke) in Trondheim, submitting the church accounts for 1617–1620. In both 1616 and 1626, he conducted official inventories of the
2 CONC  Trondheim estate alongside the lagman (lawspeaker).
2 CONT * '''Castle Law Administrator:''' Later in life, he was appointed administrator of the castle law in Trondheim, a long-held position in which he is recorded in both 1631 and 1634. In August 1631, he famously instructed the clerk t
2 CONC o notify the executioner regarding upcoming executions to be carried out in the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Wealth and Commercial Operations ===
2 CONT Jørgen Gram was a man of significant wealth with diverse business interests:
2 CONT * '''Sawmills:''' In 1622, he owned one sawmill in Strinda and two in Skogn. On February 15, 1635, he was granted the Leren sawmill (in Strinda) free of taxes for himself and his heirs, likely as royal recognition for his efforts 
2 CONC and services regarding the local copper works.
2 CONT * '''Properties:''' He owned an urban estate in Trondheim, and by 1625, he was recorded as owning 5 "spann" of rural land, most of which had been bought from fellow burghers.
2 CONT * '''Fisheries:''' He also actively participated in the lucrative herring fisheries in Bjugn.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family and Descendants ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Jørgen Gram was married to '''Inger Hansdatter''' (c. 1584 – died before 1654). She was the daughter of the parish priest in Ytterøy, Hans Johansen, and his wife NN Hansdatter Helsing.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Schønning's genealogy table, their children included:
2 CONT * '''Hans Jørgensen Gram:''' Served as an engineer at the citadel in the East Indies (Ost-Indien).
2 CONT * '''Johan Jørgensen Gram:''' Became the district judge/scribe (sorenskriver) in Strinda. He married a niece of Otto Lorch.
2 CONT * '''Margrete Jørgensdatter Gram:''' Married Christian Giertsen, who served as the town bailiff (byfogd) of Trondheim in 1645.
2 CONT * '''Karen Jørgensdatter Gram:''' Married Pouffel (Povel/Paul) Busch, who, like his brother-in-law, served as town bailiff of Trondheim in 1645.
2 CONT * '''Steen Jørgensen Gram:''' Recorded in the family lineage records.
2 CONT * '''Boel Jørgensdatter Gram:''' Married Postmaster Daniel Tagesen.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Dahl, Svein Tore (2001): ''Embetsmenn i Midt-Norge i tiden 1536-1660'', p. 29 (section "Jørgen Gram"). Utilizing sources from the Norwegian Royal Registries (NRR Vol. IV, VII, VIII) and local fief accounts. Referenced from image
2 CONC  {2C1763ED-AD2F-45E3-B340-7B346C8A4532}.jpg.
2 CONT * Schønning, Gerhard: *Slektsregister over familien Trygge* (housed at the Royal Danish Library in Copenhagen).
