1 OCCU Erkebiskop, Archevèque de Nidaros (Dronthiem, Trondheim), Erkebiskop i Nidaros, Erkebiskop i Trondhjem 1265-67, før det var han biskop i Hamar, bestyyrer av domskolen i Oslo og biskop i Oslo 1248-65, Archbishop of Nidaros; Archbi
2 CONC shop of Trondheim (1265
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Haakon Toresson==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bishop and Archbishop. Father of Tore Håkonsson (mentioned 1271-1317).
2 CONT
2 CONT * Håkon was a bishop in Oslo from 1248 and was elected Archbishop of Nidaros after Einar Smjørbak, but only managed to work for a few months before he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Håkon was a schoolmaster and a member of the chapter in the Hallvard Church in O
2 CONT
2 CONT * Haakon Toressøn. 6/6 1318, he and his sister Kristin issued a letter [1] after which their then deceased father, when he was still fresh, had often told them what he owed to Bishop Helge of Oslo and that this debt had now been p
2 CONC aid. Two priests, sira Matthæus and sira Solve, the last priest on the giraffe, witnessed this by putting their seals under the letter (Dipl. III, 13). If there is no confusion, the aforementioned Tore Haakon son would be the same
2 CONC  as Tore "bishop son", a son of Bishop Haakon in Oslo, later Archbishop, and a son-in-law by Erling Alvsson in Tornberg (dead 1283 and father of the known Alv Erlingsson1. - https://slekt.org/books/quisling/gaard1/003.html
2 CONT
2 CONT * Manages the Oslo School of Appeal, mentioned as a very trusted man by the King.  His family is believed to be from Eastern Norway, as a bishop in Oslo he owned the big farm Bjerke at Follo.  Possible that he also owned a party i
2 CONC n the farm Sjonarhuag in Trøgstda, which was later donated by his son Thore to the church. His son also inherited a lot of goods on Ragnvaldsøy in Orkney, but this goods Hedem believes must come from the mother of Thore or a frien
2 CONC d of Håkon. Who Håkon was married according to Hedem is unknown, but it is likely that she may have had the name Elin and was from Lekum.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1248-1265: Bishop in Oslo'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1267:' '' Archbishop of Nidaros'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:' '' A. E. Hedem: "Bishop's family and Manvik estate" in NST no.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Håkon - elaboration (NBL article) by Author: Audun Dybdahl==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon, death August 18, 1267, death place Trondheim, Sør-Trøndelag, Birth year and birthplace is not known. Bishop and Archbishop. Parents: Unknown. Father of Tore Håkonsson (mentioned 1271-1317).
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon was a bishop in Oslo from 1248 and was elected Archbishop of Nidaros after Einar Smjørbak, but only managed to work for a few months before he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon was a schoolmaster and member of the chapters at the Hallvard Church in Oslo when he was consecrated to the bishop there by Archbishop Sigurd 1248. When Nidaro's Archbishop's chair was vacant at Einar Smjørbak's death in 126
2 CONC 3, the canons suggested the abbot for Tautra monastery, Birger, as new archbishop. This choice was obviously not in line with King Magnus Lagabøte's wish. The saga says that when the king and the abbot met in Bergen, "they did not
2 CONC  speak much to each other". Probably the king has sought to influence the selection of the new Archbishop. The Pope at least rejected the local proposal and appointed a committee consisting of four Norwegian monastery leaders who 
2 CONC were to point out the next archbishop. The group gathered about Bishop Håkon in Oslo, a choice the king obviously was pleased with.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since Håkon was a bishop in advance, no new ordinance was needed in Rome. In 1266, the Pope gave his permission for the bishops in Bergen and Stavanger to put on the Håkon pallium and demand him the oath. The solemn consecration t
2 CONC ook place in the cathedral in Nidaro's Thursday morning (April 14) 1267. The new archbishop himself dedicated two Icelandic bishops on the same occasion. It was otherwise not great Archbishop Håkon managed to accomplish before he 
2 CONC died on August 18 the same year. Two days before his death, however, he gave the municipality's capital the revenues of the county church at Haug i Verdal to strengthen the financial basis of the cannons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources also show that Håkon, as bishop in Oslo, added a considerable piece of land to his court chapter. A common table team for the cannons in Oslo was probably created just before 1268. According to a gift letter from 1264,
2 CONC  the bishop donated several large properties to the cannons at the cathedral against their commitment to reading soul fairs for the bishop and his mother and distributing food to 30 poor and gifts to the churches in Oslo on the da
2 CONC y of the bishop's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon obviously stood well with the king. He was for example. very central to the delegation sent to Denmark to retrieve Ingeborg, Erik Plovpenning's daughter, as the young Magnus Lagabøte's bride. According to Håkon Håkonsson's s
2 CONC aga, Bishop Hakon's profit was far on the way that Ingeborg came to Norway after many complications. It also appears that the bishop's counsel was given great weight when the king decided that Magnus should be crowned in connectio
2 CONC n with the wedding.
2 CONT
2 CONT We do not know anything about Håkon's family back in time, but we know that he had a son, Tore Håkonsson (or Biskopsson), who was one of the country's most prominent men in the late 13th century as a baron and chancellor. He lived
2 CONC  at Lekum in Eidsberg in Borgesysle. The historian Edvard Bull d.e. has claimed that Hakon has probably been married; in his opinion one could hardly imagine that such a noble man as Tore could be born illegitimate. If Håkon was r
2 CONC eally married, he probably must have been the last married Catholic bishop in Norway. Something other than a guess cannot, however, be this. The fact that he was a native-born need not be a prerequisite for Tore's worldly career.
2 CONT
