2 CONT Ingeborg (no. Ingebjørg Håkonsdatter), 1301-61, norsk prinsessa, dotter till kung Håkon V Magnusson och drottning Eufemia (av Ruppin). I. trolovades som ettåring med den svenske hertigen Erik Magnusson och förmäldes, efter flera f
2 CONC örvecklingar, med honom 1312. Efter makens död 1318 kom hon att spela en betydande politisk roll i egenskap av mor till Magnus Eriksson, vilken 1319 som treåring blev kung i Sverige och Norge. Med utgångspunkt i de slott och län h
2 CONC on innehade i Västergötland, Värmland, Halland och Bohuslän bedrev hon tillsammans med den halländske riddaren Knut Porse under 1320-talets första hälft en självständig utrikespolitik i sonens namn. Detta skapade motsättningar til
2 CONC l såväl det svenska som det norska riksrådet, och det förra berövade henne 1326 både län och politiskt inflytande i Sverige. Rådet i Norge drog in I:s norska län 1327, då hon gifte sig med Knut Porse. Efter hans död (1330) tycks I
2 CONC . ha närmat sig sonen, men det är inte möjligt att avgöra om hon spelade någon politisk roll under återstoden av sitt liv. I. var även mor till bl.a. Eufemia (d. 1370).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingeborg of Norway, a.k.a Duchess Ingeborg, Old Norse Ingibjörg Hákonardóttir, Swedish Ingeborg Håkansdotter, (1301-1361), was a Scandinavian royal duchess and sometime regent of Norway (1319-1321) and Sweden (1319-1326).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingeborg was born as the only legitimate daughter of king Haakon V of Norway from his marriage with Euphemia of Rügen. As a child, she was first betrothed to Magnus Birgerson, the son and designated heir of king Birger I of Sweden
2 CONC . Soon afterwards the engagement was however broken for altered political reasons, and in 1305 she was betrothed to Eric, Duke of Södermanland, a younger brother of king Birger of Sweden, thus uncle of her first betrothed. In 1312
2 CONC , Ingeborg and Eric were formally married; at her wedding, her mother queen Euphemia had published the recently-translated (by her command) famous poems, the Euphemia songs.
2 CONT
2 CONT She barely was old enough to bear her first husband two children, in 1316 and in 1317, before Duke Eric was murdered. Her son Magnus VII of Norway, at the age of 3, was proclaimed king of Norway upon her father's death, in rights 
2 CONC devolved from her. Ingeborg was recognized as formal regent of her son in Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon, the Swedish nobility elected young Magnus king of Sweden after deposing Birger, and Ingeborg was made nominal regent of Sweden and given a seat and vote in the Swedish government and the title: Ingeborg, by the Grace of God,
2 CONC  daughter of Haakon, Duchess in the Kingdom of Sweden. However, power was mostly in hands of the two highest officers of the Swedish kingdom. Duchess Ingeborg held her own court at her residense in Varberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingeborg married her lover Knud Porse (d. 1330), a nobleman from less than royal circles, in 1327. While Knud was allowed to become Duke of Halland and holder of Ingeborg's inherited estates, her marriage was another reason why Sw
2 CONC edes, and also increasingly Norwegians, did not allow Ingeborg to use her governmental power in these kingdoms; after an intrigue was discovered, where she had made plans of taking direct control with her husband and making her po
2 CONC sitions independent, she was stripped of her power and her position as regent in Norway (1321) and Sweden (1323) - though she remained titular regent in Sweden and kept her place in the council until 1326, and had a place in the p
2 CONC arliament. Her husband was made Duke of Estonia in 1329. In 1330, she became a widow. Her younger son's became dukes of Halland. Her eldest son became an adult in 1332, and the same year, Ingeborg secured the (temporary) Swedish s
2 CONC uperiority over Skåne. In 1350, she inherited the title and position of Duke of Halland from her younger son's.
2 CONT
