1 NOTE 4TH EARL OF BOTHWELL
1 NOTE James Hepburn, Fourth Earl of Bothwell (?1536-78), husband of Mary Queen of Scots. In 1560 the queen-dowager entrusted him with a special mission to France. Recalled by the queen in 1565 to assist her in subduing Moray�s rebellion
2 CONC , he, after the murder of Rizzio in March, 1566, gradually acquired a supreme influence in her counsels; and there can be no doubt that his determination to secure her hand was the chief cause of Darnley�s murder. At the same time
2 CONC , both he and the queen were the dupes of cooler and cleverer intriguers, and his marriage rendered the ruin of both inevitable. At Carberry Hill the queen, to save Bothwell�s life, made arrangements by which he should be permitte
2 CONC d to escape. After lurking for some time in the north of Scotland, he made an attempt to establish himself in the Orkneys as a kind of pirate; but on being pursued by Kirkaldy of Grange, he escaped to Denmark, arriving at Copenhag
2 CONC en on September 30, 1567. At first he met with a favorable reception, but was never at liberty. In June, 1573, he was removed from the castle of Malmö to close imprisonment at Drangholm, in Zealand, where he died (April 14, 1578).
2 CONC  [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
