1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Solve Ormsen og hans hustru Margreta avhendet til Nes kirke ved biskop Eystein halvottende laupsland i Hem "i Loftzgarden som Midgarden er" og det var halve jorden. . Thore, prest på Saude, og to andre menn, kunngj
2 CONC ør at Solve Ormson solgte 5 markebol i Loftsgarden i Epletveit i Brunkeberg Sogn til Torevil Torgeirson.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT DN b.XI s.90
2 CONT
2 CONT Sammendrag: Thore, Prest paa Saude, og to andre Mænd kundgjöre, at Solve Ormssön solgte 5 Markebol i Loftsgaarden i Eplethveit i Brunkeberg Sogn til Thorevil Thorgeirssön og erkjendte at have oppebaaret Betalingen.
2 CONT
2 CONT b.VI s.392	
2 CONT
2 CONT Sammendrag:
2 CONT
2 CONT To Mænd kundgjöre, at Salve Ormssön, Maritte Bjarnesdatter og deres Sön
2 CONT
2 CONT Aanund gjorde et Mageskifte med Thorgeir Lidvordssön.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilde:
2 CONT
2 CONT Efter Orig. p. Perg. p. Gaarden Roaldstad i Tyrisdal. Skriften meget afslidt.Begge Segl mangle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nummer: 355.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dato: Septbr. 1399 - Septbr. 1400. Sted: [Thelemarken?]
2 CONT
2 CONT Brevtekst (fra den trykte utgaven):
2 CONT
2 CONT Ollom monnom þæim sem þetta bref sea æder hore se[n]da
2 CONT
2 CONT Eil[i]f[uer] . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . Gunnerson q. g. oc sine kunnic
2 CONT
2 CONT gerandhe at mit varom a [p]restga[rdenom] . . . . . . . . . . . [d]agen
2 CONT
2 CONT a .xi. are rikis vars virdulix herra herra Erix med gud[s nad] Norix
2 CONT
2 CONT konongs [sagom oc hordom a han]darban Saulue Ormmersonar Marritte
2 CONT
2 CONT Bienne dottor oc On[nunds sonar] þæire [aff æinne halfue en aff annare
2 CONT
2 CONT Þor]gierzs Liduorzsonar at þæir k/oe/ipte jordom. gaf fyrnemder Saulu[e Or-
2 CONT
2 CONT m]erson Ma[rritte Bienne dotter oc Onn]under sun þæir[e] Þorgæire
2 CONT
2 CONT Liduorsyni adernemdom .v. laupeland j Lopstog . . . . . . . j sw[dgar-
2 CONT
2 CONT dinom ok] . . . . . . boll j Synstease oc þwigge laupelandh j Vorugun-
2 CONT
2 CONT rudh j Ruyima(?) . . . k#zs. en Þ[orgæir Liduorzson fyr]nemder gaf Saluue
2 CONT
2 CONT Ormersyni half sette lauplan j swdgardinom j Arenesse oc þ . . . . . .
2 CONT
2 CONT . . . . . . j Nessk#zs. fræ[l]zs ok hei(m)olt hwor odrum *borædhe firir
2 CONT
2 CONT hworium manne oc þil sans [witnisburd her wm sætto]m mit ockur in
2 CONT
2 CONT sigle firir þetta bref er giort var deigi oc are sum fyr seg[ir].
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilde: Efter Orig. p. Perg. i Vidensk. Selsk.s Saml. i Throndhjem. (No. 12, fra Stiftsprovst H. J. Willes Saml.) Alle 3 Segl mangle. (Jfr. Dipl. Norv. IV No. 651 og 1016; VI No. 355; IX No. 758 og 760).
2 CONT
2 CONT Nummer: 105.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dato: Sept. 1400-Sept. 1401.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sted: [Saude.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Brevtekst (fra den trykte utgaven):
2 CONT
2 CONT Ollum monnum þeim som þetta bref sia æder høræ sendæ Þorer pre(s)ter a Saudum Solui Ormarson ok Rorlæiuer Biornson :q: g: ok sinæ kunnikt gerande at a tolftæ are vars verdulix herra b.XI s.91 Erix med [gudz1 nad Norex konongz hørd
2 CONC om ok *sadom at Soluir Ormarson seldi Þoreuildi Þorgeirs/son/ v mar(ka) bool .j. Æpplæzwetum frelst ok hemult firir *þiariom manne med lutum ok lunnidum som tilliger ok leget hauer fra forno ok nygiu vten garzs ok jnnan ok som lig
2 CONC ger .j.j. Brvggæbergæsokn ok þo .j.j. Lofgardenom .j.j. fyr nemdæ sokn kiendiz þa firirnemder Solui at han hafde vpboret firstæ penig(e)n ok æf(s)tæ som .j.j. kaup þeiræ kom. til [sanende1 setom mit okor jnsigli meder Solues firne
2 CONC mdom firir þetta bref er gort var degi ok are som fir sægir
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Tilskrevet over Linien. tilbake
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.dokpro.uio.no/perl/middelalder/diplom_vise_tekst.prl?b=6088&s=n&str=
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He belonged to the lower nobility. 1 June 1442 he was one of 36 delegates from “the ordinary people” to a national meeting confirming the election of Kristoffer of Bavaria as the new King of Norway,  Sweden and Denmark. His name 
2 CONC Solve means “solved” or “saved”. The tradition is that it was given  to him because he survived the great plague The Black Death of 1349/50; just after he was born.  Another version is that it was his mother who survived the plagu
2 CONC e, and that he was born prematurely just afterwards, but nevertheless grew up. Since he was an active man as late as in 1442, I find it  less likely that he was born as early as ca. 1350. Hence, I must assume that if the traditio
2 CONC n has a core  of truth, it must be about one of the plagues in the decades after 1350. His ancestry here is based  on the tradition, that is supported by known relations in the following generations.
