2 CONT Foreign village. Manor near Falu town in Kopparberg parish, has sometimes belonged to the crown, sometimes to individuals. In 1447 a Wilkin Olsson is named as owner, 1490 Per SeS'resson to Främmandeby, which is considered the prog
2 CONC enitor of the noble family Svinhufvud af Qvalsta, and whose wife, Barbro Johansdotter or Ligeraarsdotter, bore 2 stag horns in a blue field. The mountain man Ped. Siverdsson held the farm in 1542; in 1548 it was owned by Jöns and 
2 CONC Daniel Thorkilssons, but from 1552 to 1557 under freehold of the con. Gustaf 1, who stayed at the farm when he visited Kopparberget, for which they had set up a "gentleman's building with 7 staves and 39 windows". Aren 15 57 and 1
2 CONC 561 the farm was granted to a Jöran Persson at Risholm, called the king's troraan, and came after him to several others, but was reduced in 168 2 as a royal farm and was assigned in 1694 to the county governor on his salary. In 17
2 CONC 3 5, V3 of the farm was nevertheless restored to the councilor 01. Persson Forsslind, and in 17 52 a tax letter was issued for the rest, so that the farm was then in private hands. Daniel Thorkilsson to Främdeby was ennobled by Ki
2 CONC ng Erik and had a boar's head for his coat of arms; it is said that Carl IX gave him Lily in Aspeboda parish as a godfather's gift. One of his sons was 100 years old when he died; another, Erik Danielsson, was a mountain man, the 
2 CONC third, Jöns Danielsson, was a commander in Dalarne, and the fourth, Daniel, became a parish priest in Wika. One of Daniel's wives was the dean of Leksand, Elof Terserus. Daniel Thorkilsson's widow was then married to Christopher O
2 CONC lsson, a mountain man, his son was ennobled by Gustaf the Great, but according to the story gave the king 20 skS cups to be struck out of the noble register, on which his farms would go to his daughters with the first marriage and
2 CONC  not fall under the crown . See Hiilpher's Valley Journey, p. 337 and 436.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =The Common Origin of the Svinhufvud Families Abstract, by Anders Winroth=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000002668687302&photo_id=6000000196974252838&position=0 '''Page 1''']
2 CONT
2 CONT However, they used a heraldic crest containing a boar's head, which apparently gave rise to the family name. Where did they get this weapon from? The progenitor of the Västergötland family, Daniel Torkelsson, is thought to have ta
2 CONC ken up the coat of arms from his mother-in-law Margareta Hansdotter, who belonged to the so-called older pig head family. This family had been one of the leading salvation families at Kopparberget during the 1400’s. Its lineage is
2 CONC  usually traced back to a Jöns "swinshwow" mentioned in 1386. He must have been Margaret's great-grandfather. 4 4 The best overview of the genealogy of the older pig head family is Boëthius, Kopparbergslagen, especially pp. 573-57
2 CONC 7. Hans Gillingstam, "Svinhuvud och Svinakula", Personal history journal 64 (1966), pp. 3-7, points out some important inaccuracies in Boëthius' genealogy. Cf. also Bertil Boëthius, "Svinhuvud og Vinakula: Continued discussion", P
2 CONC ersonal history journal 64 (1966), pp. 120-123. Medieval stained glass with the pig's head coat of arms in Torsäng's church, Dalarna, now in the Historical Museum, Stockholm. Photo: National Antiquities Office.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000002668687302&photo_id=6000000196974253834&position=2 '''Page 2''']
2 CONT
2 CONT The progenitor of the Qvalstad family, Per Hansson, apparently took the pig's head in his coat of arms from his grandfather, the 1534 mountain bailiff and district chief Mäns Nilsson in Aspeboda. His father, the mountain bailiff N
2 CONC ils Mänsson (died between 1500 and 1504) also carried a pig's head in the coat of arms, but then nothing is known about his ancestors or why he used the pig's head coat of arms. "Their possible [entuall kinship with the widely bra
2 CONC nched Dalas family with the same coat of arms [.] has not been able to be investigated." 5 However, there is a hint of kinship, in that in the 1490S, Nils Mänsson together with Margit Gudmundsdotter, who was the widow of a member 
2 CONC of the older pig head family, filed a joint birthright lawsuit. Assuming that Margit sued on behalf of her children as she did in at least one other case), Nils must have been related to her husband's family, the Pig's Head family
2 CONC . 6 A parchment letter from 1493 in the Riksarkivet gives a clue as to how Nils Mänsson was related to the older pig head family. The letter is unfortunately damaged by moisture and partially illegible, even under ultraviolet ligh
2 CONC t. However, its content is beyond doubt. When the lawman for Västmanland and Dalarna Knut Eskilsson (Banér) held a lawmansting in Västeras (in the presence of, among other distinguished men, the national superintendent Sten Sture)
2 CONC  Nils Mánsson appeared on Kopparberget and complained that a certain Anders Larson in "Giäse" (probably Gäsjö in Norberg's parish ) had acquired a total of 2⅔ acres of land in the villages of Rysta and Bellsta, Odensvi parish, Äke
2 CONC rbo county. The lawyer, however, rejected Nils' claim after Anders had proved by presenting both a deed of sale and a deed of fast that his "parents" had bought the disputed land from a "mongx" Henriksson for 110 Stockholm coins. 
2 CONC The lawman's verdict says nothing about who this Mäns or Magnus was, but the conclusion is close at hand that he was Nils Mansson's father. 5 The documents mentioned in this article thus solve the old riddle about how the two intr
2 CONC oduced Svinhufud families are related. Their common origin must already be sought in the early 1400S. The coat of arms that gave them their name was inherited several times through the female line, something that was fairly common
2 CONC  in the Middle Ages but has confused genealogists ever since. 11 APPENDIX: Edition of the 1493 lawman's judgment The lawman's judgment of 1493 has never been printed, and there are, as far as I know, no modern copies. The research
2 CONC er who wants to read its text is thus referred to the original in the National Archives, which is difficult to read due to moisture damage. To assist interested researchers, I am printing here my transcript of the letter, partiall
2 CONC y done with the help of ultraviolet light. The transcript is provided with the reservation that I am no expert in medieval Swedish, and I have probably been guilty of mistakes in details. I thank Professor Per-Axel Wiktorsson, who
2 CONC  kindly gave me good advice on the art of publishing texts in Swedish. The printer who printed the letter made extensive use of abbreviations. I've highlighted in italics the letters I added when breaking down the abbreviations. I
2 CONC n brackets are words that could not be read due to the damage, but which I thought I could guess. Three points in brackets [.] mark places where I could neither read nor guess. The slashes / (comma) mark the punctuation marks foun
2 CONC d in the original. I have added periods at the end of some sentences to make reading easier.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000002668687302&photo_id=6000000196974253835&position=1 '''Page 3''']
2 CONT
2 CONT The letter is not transcribed in Johan Peringskiöld's large transcription work, but his assistant C. R. Berch has written a summary in Diarium novum Peringskiöldianum III, p. 2286 (in the Vitterhetsakademien's deposit in the Natio
2 CONC nal Archives). The letter seems to have been damaged even then, as Berch could not read the year either and his information about the number of herbage in the dispute is incorrect. Kröningsvärd read the year as "mcdxliv", thus 144
2 CONC 4, but Tom Söderberg, without explanation, miscalculated the letter to 1450, see Stora Kopparberget und medeltiden and Gustav Vasa, Stockholm 1932, p. 230, n. 3. At my request, the state herald Henrik Klackenberg kindly informed m
2 CONC e that the year in the letter is written "mcd×lx", which explains Söderberg's dating.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, it is hard to believe that the writer of the letter would have chosen such a complicated way of writing 1450. Either there is a typing error (for 1449?), or the scribe started writing 1449 (mcdxlix) because he forgot that
2 CONC  it was already 1450, but corrected himself before writing the last characters. ANDeRS WINROH, b. 1965, file. dr, assistant professor of history at Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut, USA. Was 1987-1991 editor at Swedish Biog
2 CONC raphical Lexicon. Has written the books Stockholms stadsarkiv and slätforskaren. A tutorial for researchers in Stockholm's family and personal history (SSGF's writing series, 2;
2 CONT 1991) and The Making of Gratian's Decretum (Cambridge 2000), as well as several articles on historical, genealogical and biographical topics.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11 Cf. Hans Gillingstam, "Could one in the Middle Ages take up arms from his wife's family?" Personal history journal 58 (1960), pp. 84-87. 12 The tens and ones in the year are thus not legible. That the letter was issued in 1493,
2 CONC  however, is evident from the fact that another letter was issued on the same day ( Odensdagen nest aepter nativitate marie ) 1493 in Västeras and sealed by several people who also sealed the lawman's judgment, see Riksarkivet, pa
2 CONC per letter I, 1 1 September 1493. 13 "nils" repeated by mistake. 14 According to Kröningsvärd's in and of itself unreliable edition of Magnus Henriksson's deed of sale, only "half-full" örügland was sold here. 15 "had" added in th
2 CONC e margin.
2 CONT
2 CONT =[https://www.academia.edu/15062386/Svinhufvud_%C3%A4tternas_gemensamma_ursprungemail_work_card=thumbnail The Common Origin of the Svinhufvud Families, By Anders Winroth]
2 CONT
2 CONT One of perhaps the most eye-catching Swedish noble names is Pig's head,  carried by two ancient introduced lineages: Pig's head by Qvalstad (Nr.145) and Svinhufvud in Västergötland ( Nr.199). That the latter's principal branch cha
2 CONC nged its name, first to Qvist and then to Nordqvist, perhaps testifies to a certain doubt concerning the appropriateness of the name. The main branch was also subjected to estate circulation, with the result that the nobility was 
2 CONC forgotten, and it was only twenty years ago that PontusMöller was able to introduce this branch to the genealogies of the House of Knights. However, other members of the family seem to have worn the name with pride, such as, for e
2 CONC xample, the ﬁ the Finnish president Pehr (Pekka) Evind Svinhufvud af Qvalstad; af Qvalstad; (1861- 1944).  Both families originate from Kopparberget in Dalarna, and one would imagine that they have a common origin. However, this c
2 CONC ommon origin has so far eluded research. Gustaf Elgenstierna's review from (at least in this regard).  than that the noble progenitor of the family Svinhufvud in Västergötland, Daniel Torkelsson, was married to a cousin of the nob
2 CONC le progenitor of the family Svinhufvud of Qvalstad Peder Hansson or granddaughter of the latter's grandfather Peder Jönsson Skrifvare.
2 CONT
2 CONT The noble progenitors, who lived in the 1500-talet century and both were ennobled by Johan III, did not use the family name that their descendants adopted 1600-talet century, but they were known by first names and patronymics like
2 CONC  most of their brothers. However, they used a heraldic coat of arms containing a boar's head, which apparently gave rise to the family name. Where did they get this weapon from? The progenitor of the Västergötland family Daniel To
2 CONC rkelsson must have taken the weapon from his mother-in-law Margareta Hansdotter , which belonged to the so-called older pig's head family. This family had been one of the leading salvation families at Kopparberget under 1400- Tale
2 CONC t the number. Its lineage is usually traced back to one  Jön's "swinshwow" as mentioned He must have been Margareta's great-grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://docplayer.se/69116463-Tal-hallet-vid-lunch-med-atten-svinhufvud-af-qvalstad-pa-riddarhuset-i-stockholm-den-29-september-2013.html Talk held at lunch with the family Svinhufvud af Qvalstad at Riddarhuset in Stockholm on 
2 CONC September 29, 2013]
2 CONT
2 CONT Spoke at lunch with the family Svinhufvud af Qvalstad at Riddarhuset in Stockholm on September 29, 2013 By Johan A. Lundberg secretary of the Family Association SVINHUFVUD in Westergötland Ladies and gentlemen! This weekend you ha
2 CONC ve made an excursion into your early family history, a family history that to a large extent is also our early family history. You have made an excursion back to the roots of Kopparbergslagen in Dalarna. In the Svinhufvud Family A
2 CONC ssociation in Westergötland, we arranged a similar excursion in 1999 which was much appreciated. After this weekend, you probably know everything about the Svinhufvud families' partly common origins in Dalarna a long time ago, so 
2 CONC I will not dwell on that. I have instead been asked by Pehr Sommar to update you with some information about your enraged siblings in Sweden, the family Svinhufvud in Westergötland, No. 199. This family today officially consists o
2 CONC f two branches: a main branch, whose future existence is clearly endangered, and a younger branch, which is slightly less endangered. Daniel Torkelsson (Torkilsson). Miner at Stora Kopparberget. Noble Married 1st with Anna Jönsdot
2 CONC ter, who on her mother descended from a late medieval family circle in Dalarna who carried pig heads. Jöns Danielsson Fogde. (died 1613) Married to Christina Larsdotter (von Köllen) Göran Jönsson Svinhufvud (died 1639) Huvudmannag
2 CONC ren (Qvist / Nordqvist) Carl Jönsson Svinhufvud (died 1636) Younger branch (Svinhufvud) which on the mother descended from a late medieval family circle in Dalarna who carried pigs' main weapons. Jöns Danielsson Fogde. (died 1613)
2 CONC  Married to Christina Larsdotter (von Köllen) Göran Jönsson Svinhufvud (died 1639) Huvudmannagren (Qvist / Nordqvist) Carl Jönsson Svinhufvud (died 1636) Younger branch (Svinhufvud) which on the mother descended from a late mediev
2 CONC al family circle in Dalarna who carried pigs' main weapons. Jöns Danielsson Fogde. (died 1613) Married to Christina Larsdotter (von Köllen) Göran Jönsson Svinhufvud (died 1639) Huvudmannagren (Qvist / Nordqvist) Carl Jönsson Svinh
2 CONC ufvud (died 1636) Younger branch (Svinhufvud)
2 CONT
2 CONT
