2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Christopher Olsson (Gullenknoster) PE Nor, adi. 1021, 1628, 2. 3:0 
2 CONT * A brother of Kristofer Olsson (Gyllenknoster) was Nils Olsson of Medola in Finland (see Bo?thius, p. 435), who became a warrior and was ennobled in 1563, and was executed in 1599 as a follower of Sigismund (see Bo?thius, p. 435 
2 CONC and Almquist, p. 179).  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: (1)  '''Malin Knutsdotter,''' died 1595 29/6, daughter of the bailiff at Kopparberget and the mountain man in Varggården Knut Persson. 
2 CONT * Married:(2)  '''Britta Månsdotter (Roos af Hjelmsäter),''' Married 2nd "as early as 1601 20/1" to Brita Månsdotter (Roos af Hjelmsäter), died 1614 24/7, widow of the mountain man Daniel Torkelsson in Främby (ennobled Svinhufvud 
2 CONC in Westergötland). 
2 CONT * Married: (3) to '''Karin Andersdotter,''' daughter of miner Anders Räf in Sundborn, remarried to miner Johan Eriksson in Hyttnäs. Schlegel & Klingspor's information about Kristofer Olsson's children is hardly to be trusted. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://forum.rotter.se/index.php?topic=66503.0 Gyllenknoster Updated Geneology Research, by Torsten Berglund]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT My article about the bailiff Jöran Olsson (Stjärna, Kopparbergsätten) mentions the Gyllenknoster family only for the reason that Jöran Olsson and several other members of his family have been wrongly placed in the Gyllenknoster fa
2 CONC mily genealogy in Schlegel & Klingspor, The one with shield letters granted but not to the knight house introduced genealogical charts of the Swedish nobility (1875).
2 CONT
2 CONT You will thus find in Schlegel & Klingspor a genealogy of "Kristofer Olofssons till Nor adel." ätt (Gyllenknoster)" (p. 156-159) which, however, for the most part instead occupies members of the family active at Kopparberget who c
2 CONC arried a six-pointed star in the coat of arms. Already the information about who was the father of the noble mountain man Kristofer Olsson (Gyllenknoster) to Nor is incorrect with Schlegel&Klingspor, who in turn took their genealo
2 CONC gical information from Kröningsvärd's infamous and imaginative genealogical tables in Diplomatarium Dalekarlicum (1853). The incorrect and mixed-up genealogies of the star family and Gyllenknoster at Kröningsvärd and Schlegel&Klin
2 CONC gspor have unfortunately also been the basis for Jan Raneke's account of the star family in a work as late as Svenska medeltidsvapen (1982),  
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also important to point out that the mountain man Kristofer Olsson (Gyllenknoster) in Nor has been mixed up in the older literature with his namesake, the mountain master and mountain bailiff at Kopparberget Kristofer Olsson
2 CONC  (Stjärna, Kopparbergsätten) in Korsnäs (mentioned 1500-1531). From this I guess that also the literary statement (among other things by the Elgenstiers, noble family Anckarström) that Kristofer Olsson (Gyllenknoster) in Nor wrote
2 CONC  himself to Forssnäs (=Korsnäs?) arose.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In Bertil Bo? thius's work, the Kopparbergslagen until the breakthrough of the 1570s. Upkkomst, medeltid, tidig vasatid (1965), the most important work regarding the knowledge of people and family relationships at Kopparberget dur
2 CONC ing the labeled time, there is information about the mountain man Kristofer Olsson's correct family relationships to Nor (p. 435). He was the son of the mountain man Olof Jönsson in Born at Kopparberget who is mentioned in the ann
2 CONC ual interest and in the even numbers there 1538-44. In 1545, Olof Jönsson relinquished his farm in Born to the crown's own farm that was established in Born in the 1530s, and instead he acquired a farm in Främsbacka from the star 
2 CONC family. In 1556/57 he served as the crown's bailiff at Garpenberg. Olof Jönsson's wife, who was the daughter of the miner Erik Jonsson in Uggleviken, was remarried to Nils Jonsson who lived in Främsbacka in 1571.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Bo?thius assumes that Olof Jönsson was the son of the Jöns Olsson in Born who in 1533 was found among Gustav I's followers at Kopparberget and who in turn may be identical to the Jöns Olsson who in 1529-31 was the crown's bailiff 
2 CONC at Kopparberget (p 211 -212, 273; see also Almquist, local administration, part 4, p. 115). 
2 CONT
2 CONT A brother of Kristofer Olsson (Gyllenknoster) was Nils Olsson of Medola in Finland (see Bo?thius, p. 435), who became a warrior and was ennobled in 1563, and was executed in 1599 as a follower of Sigismund (see Bo?thius, p. 435 an
2 CONC d Almquist, p. 179).  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Schlegel&Klingspor, in 1608 Kristofer Olsson bought the farm Nor at Kopparberget (something about Forssnäs is not mentioned), he was one of the leading mountaineers and "left the crown great advances", was churchwarde
2 CONC n at Kopparberget and a member of the House of Commons in 1604, and was ennobled in 1621 30/1 by Gustav II Adolf with a weapon which, according to the shield letter quoted in Schlegel&Klingspor, contained "a blue mountain man with
2 CONC  a yellow fiddle with his burning lamp on his head, holding in his left hand a hook set in the mountain and in his right hand a club in full swing, to drive the hook and break the mountain. Ofwan on top of a lantern Tornerhielm an
2 CONC d on the hielmen tu knoster with guhlskafft in a cross". However, he used a simpler weapon, two crossed stone irons, according to a description of a weapon that used to hang in Stora Kopparberg's church. A now lost epitaph that Kr
2 CONC istofer set up in Stora Kopparberg's church in 1618 had portraits of him and his deceased two wives as well as five sons and three daughters who were also deceased (see Sweden churches, Dalarna, vol II:1, Falun, p 67). He must hav
2 CONC e died without sons and ended his family himself on the side of the sword (before 1638).  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Schlegel&Klingspor, Kristofer Olsson was married 1st to Malin Knutsdotter, died 1595 29/6, daughter of the bailiff at Kopparberget and the mountain man in Varggården Knut Persson. Married 2nd "as early as 1601 20/1" t
2 CONC o Brita Månsdotter (Roos af Hjelmsäter), died 1614 24/7, widow of the mountain man Daniel Torkelsson in Främby (ennobled Svinhufvud in Westergötland). Married 3rd to Karin Andersdotter, daughter of miner Anders Räf in Sundborn, re
2 CONC married to miner Johan Eriksson in Hyttnäs. Schlegel&Klingspor's information about Kristofer Olsson's children is hardly to be trusted. They mention three daughters and a son from the first marriage, as well as a son Hans from an 
2 CONC unknown marriage who probably does not belong here at all. The son from the first marriage is said to have been called Karl and to have died in 1627 in Livland as a lieutenant in the Dal regiment, but the personal history of the D
2 CONC al regiment, volume 1 (1984), p 209, have not been able to identify him. The three daughters were Elisabet, married to Klas Depken, died 1634, citizen of Västerås and miner in Noret, whose son Klas Depken (1627-1702) was ennobled 
2 CONC in 1676 with the name Anckarström (see further Elgenstierna, vol. 1, p. 121); Kristina, died 1630, married in 1610 to Simon Simonsson Depken (1586-1625) in Västerås; NN, married to mountain man Anders Eriksson in Korsnäs at Koppar
2 CONC berget.  
2 CONT
2 CONT However, according to mountain councilman Jacob Ingelsson's account of Stora Kopparberget in 1716 (ed. by Helmer Lagergren 1913), Kristofer Olsson had only two daughters, namely Klas Depken's wife and Anders Eriksson i Korsnäs' wi
2 CONC fe, both from his first marriage to the daughter of Knut Persson i Varggården (who was grandfather's father to Jakob Ingelsson). According to Ingelsson, no children were born in the second marriage and with the third wife "he had 
2 CONC some children, but they all died". In the article about Simon Depken (the younger) in Svenskt biografiskt lexikon, volume 11 (1945) p. 100, it is said that his wife Kristina Kristofersdotter was not the daughter of Kristofer Olsso
2 CONC n in Nor, an incorrect 18th-century statement, but of a burgher in Västerås.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://archive.org/details/MedDallvenFrrnKLlornaTillHavetDelIIIBok4/page/n157/mode/2up?q=Knut Med Dalälven Från Källorna Till Havet Del III Bok 4 Stora Kopparberg, by Karl Erik Forsslund]
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000220251526823&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000220251653894&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik Skinnare sold to Stora Nissa 14491) ; another quarry Baambara arfwit wihr Borem (oden part of the property Norslund, which is located at the North, where Tisken flows into Runn>). as underlagman Korske exchanged for him by In
2 CONC gewalde 1 norrena and silde to Dalafogden Jösse Finsson 1400%): eat a tikt Dafinsarwit üstan Erik Skinnare sold to Stora Nissa 14491) ; another quarry Baambara arfwit wihr Borem (oden part of the property Norslund, which is locate
2 CONC d at the North, where Tisken flows into Runn>). as underlagman Korske exchanged for him by Ingewalde 1 norrena and silde to Dalafogden Jösse Finsson 1400%): eat a tikt Dafinsarwit üstan tiskisse oden part of Kungsgärden Norets ego
2 CONC r invid Falu stad, sik-sar om Tisken>), which Dagfind Dagfindsson, bergsman 1351, ürft after his father, who cultivated it E. F. 1925 - he was one of the Germans who during the 12th and 14th centuries practiced IMAGE 181. HARALDSB
2 CONC O, MANBYGGNADEN'S EDGE. mining at Kopparberget.
2 CONT Dagfind d. y's daughter Walburgh and her husband Jowan Dafinzmager (måg) or Jon Daghfintz i daghfinzarweno i nore mortgaged the property in 1400 to Jüsse Finsson, who received a fastebrer dirpa in 1406*): they also owed him copper
2 CONC s and money, which he paid from their vineyards to Lafrinze ilyttokiempason and Hindrik Symbla *). '''The name occurs later than a gang, in a salubrious book in 1528 by Anders Olsson l Noret, which lists, among other things, the D
2 CONC affijnsserwet to the judge Per Sijwardsson at Frümby and his wife Barbara (Hjort af Ornäs), whom Anders calls mijn nesthe frenka*). According to a purchase and two fastebrer from 1400, all in Grycksbo gärdsarkix. Jöns Wibförnsson 
2 CONC and his children Ingebjörn and Gudlög then sold a farm at Noret on Kopparberget to Margwart Frowloff, whom we met before at Korsnüs: the deed of sale is dated elocka odhensdagh, i.e. Dymmelonsdagen in the Easter recess, when the c
2 CONC hurch bells' clappers are tied so as not to make a sound*).
2 CONT And Kzetilbiörn in Noor is mentioned as a twelve-year-old good home in a parchment document of 1414, also in the Grycksbo archive.''' In 1539, Erik Alffsson and Nils in Noredt were fourth-party owners, the former in the 1510s
2 CONT also a judge and a seer (his grandson with the same name was ennobled Ikorn). In 1549, Hustru Karljn / Noredt is mentioned, in 1569 a farm Lunden nira täktekarls. and the mining estate of No-ret. In 1571 Päuel Mänsson, Anders Pers
2 CONC son, Lasse Oloffsson, Hinrlick Finne taxed. Wife Elin and Wife Anna in Norett - the latter had gold, silver, copper, müissing, tin. + cows and 13 goats, all valued at 732 marks 3 üre. 1612 taxpayers were Hans and
2 CONT east 1 Nor: Jöns 1 Nore (Jöns Persson, syneman 1614) and Ingel & Nohret each left his sölfskied, Clas Döpken a sölfkosa and Christopher Olsson in Nohret S R:dal:r. The "noble mining estate" Christopher Olofsson in Nohr is mentione
2 CONC d above (son of bailiff Olof Jonsson at Borns Hyttegärd and brother of Nils Olofsson at Främsbacka), b. 1510, churchwarden, great mining entrepreneur, had privileges from the King to mint his own copper, attended the lord's day in
2 CONC  Stockholm in 1604, bought the property Nohr from the miner Per Larsson in 1608, made donations to Kopparberg's church as well as to Sundborn's*) and was ennobled in 16219) D. D. 105. The Hena or Heynesarvet estate lies a good dis
2 CONC tance into the present-day town of Falu, see the first book.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the nickname Gyllen-knosters because he gave advances to the crown and admonished and commanded others to do the same, as it is stated in his long royal charter'), which according to Hülpher's notes was the only evidence avai
2 CONC lable about the Bergs Adelen in Sweden": there he speaks of Bergsadell in the past and his ancestors and his shield is established: a blue mountaineer in a yellow field with his burning lamp on his head, in his left hand a hook K.
2 CONC  B. F. 1925 PICTURE 182. KALKBOD WITH STRAW ROOF, HARALDSBO. wa on the hook and break the mountain, and on the helmet three knösters with a gold handle in a cross". However, he never took an introduction - if a savior died without
2 CONC  male heirs, his estate would go to the crown, so he renounced the nobility, but had the coat of arms attached with a gold chain to the crossbeam in Kopparberg church, from which it was, however, taken down when the choir was expa
2 CONC nded in 1685. On a pillar in the church he also erected his epitaph in 1618 - he himself died in 1628 -, where Wälachtad Christopher Olofsson in Noohr is depicted with his two first wives, 5 sons and 3 daughters, all deceased*). T
2 CONC he first wife Malin, born in 1595, was the daughter of the commander of Born's Hyttegärd Persson in Varggärden, the second was Daniel Thorkilsson Swinhufwud's widow in Frim-by Brita Mänsdotter (Roos af Hjelmsäter), 1 1614 - the th
2 CONC ird's name letters are found on the epitaph »foot zirat«: there it says ›Christopher O. S. and C. A. D. 1618», which is consistent with both Jacob Ingelsson's and Hammarström's as well as Schlegel-Klingspor's information, that Chr
2 CONC istopher Olsson's third and last wife was Carin Andersdotter Räf from Hyttnäs in Sundborn, then remarried to the mountaineer Johan Eriksson there and with him mother of assessor Johan Silfverstöm's wife - on Johan Briksson's grave
2 CONC stone in Sundborn's church his wife is also named Carin Andersdotter, † 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate register after Christopher Olsson includes, among other things: a. then planting hill on eight posts. So a small plant nursery for raising seedlings for
2 CONT 1) Printed by Schlegel cch Klingspor p. 158, where he is incorrectly stated as the son of Olof Christophersson at Rottneby as well as in D. D., Geneal. Tab, and eni. them in my Flodabok.
2 CONT 3) Schlegel-Klingspor's list of children, according to which Nigra should have lived after
2 CONT 1618, must therefore be partly incorrect. Hans Christophersson was thus not the son of Christopher Olsson, but of Bergson Christopher Hansson 1 Nygärden 1 Wika (according to Helmer Lagergren).
2 CONT
