2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre beskriver kong Sigurd slik[5] «han ble en villstyring og uvøren i alle ferd [...] en stor og sterk mann, med et kjekt utseende; han hadde brunt hår og stygg munn, men ellers vakkert ansikt. Han stod over andre i å tale godt
2 CONC  og dugelig». Den "stygge" munnen er av mange blitt tolket dit hen at han hadde hareskår.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd Ble konge i Norge da han var 4år gammel, og han var konge fra 1137 til han ble myrdet i 1155. Sigurd Munn hadde mange friller, og med en av dem fikk an datteren Helga.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd II Munn, eller Sigurd Haraldsson (født 1133, død 1155) var sønn av kong Harald Gille, og ble som treåring kåret til konge på Øretinget i Trøndelag etter kong Haralds død i desember 1136. Sigurd døde 22 år gammel, uten nevne
2 CONC verdig politisk ettermæle, men med et rykte som rundbrenner som mange senere «turboprinser» kan misunne ham.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd Haraldsson or Sigurd Munn (old Norse Sigurðr Haraldsson (1133–1155) was king of Norway from 1136 to 1155. He was son of Harald Gille, king of Norway and his mistress Tora Guttormsdotter (Þóra Guthormsdóttir). He ruled as co
2 CONC -ruler with his brothers, Inge Haraldsson and Eystein Haraldsson. His epithet Munn means "the Mouth" in Old Norse. He was killed in the power-struggle against his brother, Inge, in an early stage of the civil war era in Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father was murdered by the pretender Sigurd Slembe in 1136, Sigurd was made king at the thing of Eyrathing. At the same time, his brothers Inge and Magnus were also made kings and co-rulers. Their respective guardians joi
2 CONC ned forces against Sigurd Slembe and his ally, the former king Magnus the Blind. The battles against these pretenders dominated the early years of Sigurd's reign. In 1139, they were defeated and slain at the battle of Hvaler.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this followed a period of peace. During the minority of the brothers, Sigurd, Inge and Magnus, the Norwegian nobility (the lendmenn) cooperated to rule the kingdom and advise the kings. In 1142, their brother Eystein came to
2 CONC  Norway from Scotland. His parentage was accepted, since Harald Gille had acknowledged that he had a son overseas. Eystein thus became king and co-ruler together with Sigurd and Inge. Magnus, of whom little more is known, died of 
2 CONC natural causes at some point in the 1140s.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1152, Norway was visited by the papal legate Nicholas Breakspear. During his visit, the church in Norway was organised into a separate archbishopric, with its seat at Nidaros.
2 CONT
2 CONT As they grew up, and their old advisors died, hostility began to grow among the brothers. In 1155, all three of them were set to meet in Bergen in an effort to keep the peace. Inge accused Sigurd and Eystein of planning to have hi
2 CONC m dethroned. Sigurd denied the accusations, but a few days later one of Inge's guards was killed by one of Sigurd's. At the advice of his mother Ingrid and his senior advisor, Gregorius Dagsson, Inge ordered his men to assault the
2 CONC  house where Sigurd was residing. Sigurd had but few men, and no mercy was given. King Sigurd fell on 6 February 1155. He was buried by the old cathedral of Bergen, in what is today Bergenhus Fortress This cathedral was demolished
2 CONC  and replaced by a larger cathedral soon after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT King Eystein was late in arriving for the meeting, and only approached the city after Sigurd was already dead. An uneasy settlement was reached between Inge and Eystein, but peace between them did not last long. As it turned out, 
2 CONC the killing of king Sigurd started the second phase of the Norwegian civil war era, with fighting continuing with only short let-ups until 1208. The reasons for the fighting in Bergen remain disputed. According to the sagas, Eyste
2 CONC in and Sigurd had plotted to strip Inge of his royal title and divide his share of the kingdom between them. Some modern historians doubt this version, seeing it as Inge’s excuse for his own aggressive actions.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the following civil wars, several royal pretenders claimed to be the son of King Sigurd. For some, this was probably mostly a political statement, as royal lineage was necessary to be a candidate for the throne. Sverre Sigu
2 CONC rdsson was the most successful by far of these claimants, and eventually succeeded in becoming king of Norway. Sigurd never married.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sagas draw a rather negative picture of both Sigurd and his brother Eystein, generally choosing to portray Inge as the just ruler of the three brothers. Heimskringla writes of Sigurd:
2 CONT
2 CONT When King Sigurd grew up he was a very ungovernable, restless man in every way; and so was King Eystein, but Eystein was the more reasonable of the two. King Sigurd was a stout and strong man, of a brisk appearance; he had light b
2 CONC rown hair, an ugly mouth; but otherwise a well-shaped countenance. He was polite in his conversation beyond any man, and was expert in all exercises.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sverris saga, the saga of Sigurd's alleged son, also draws a somewhat unfavourable picture of Sigurd, contrasting him with the positive qualities of Sverre.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Haakon (Hákon), known as Haakon the Broadshouldered (1147–1162). Made king by Sigurd and Eystein's supporters after Eystein's fall in 1157, in opposition to Inge Haraldsson. Killed in battle against Inge's old supporters and their
2 CONC  new king Magnus Erlingsson. Mother: Tora (Þóra). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd (Sigurðr), known as Sigurd Markusfostre (died 1163). Proclaimed king by Haakon the Broadshouldered's followers in 1162, captured and decapitated by king Magnus' supporters in 1163. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald (Haraldr), (died 1170s). Captured and executed by king Magnus' supporters, because his parentage made him a potential threat to Magnus' rule. Mother: Kristin Sigurdsdotter (Kristín Sigurðardóttir). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sverre? (Sverrir), (died 1202). Ruled as king of Norway from 1184 until his death. Mother: Gunnhild. Whether he was in fact a son of Sigurd is highly dubious. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirik? (Eiríkr) (died 1190). Made jarl by king Sverre. Poisoned. Whether he was in fact a son of Sigurd is unknown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecilia (died late 1180s). Married Folkvid the Lawspeaker, marriage later annulled. Remarried Bård Guttormsson (Bárðr Guthormsson) 
2 CONT
